good morning I'm delighted to join Dean  Miller in welcoming the class of 2015 to  Yale I want to welcome also the  relatives and friends who have  accompanied you here and especially your  parents as a father of four college  graduates I know how proud you you  parents are of your children's  achievement and how hopeful you are for  their future and how many concerns large  and small that you have at this moment  so let me try to reassure you your  children are going to love it here and I  expect that you're going to enjoy your  association with Yale as well whether  you are a returning graduate of one of  the or or whether you're one of the vast  majority of parents who never set foot  in new haven until your children started  to think about where to go to college  you may take comfort in learning that  surveys have shown that Yale parents are  the most satisfied in the Ivy League so  welcome to Yale family we're so pleased  to have your children with us and we'll  do our best to provide them with  abundant opportunities to learn and  thrive in the four years ahead and to  provide shelter from the storm in the  next 48 hours NTU class of 2015 I make  the same pledge for you these next four  years will be a time of opportunity  unlike any other here you are surrounded  by astonishing resources fascinating  fellow students from all over the world  a learning and caring faculty intimate  residential college communities a  magnificent library to extraordinary art  museums an outstanding Museum of Natural  History superb athletic facility  and student organizations covering every  conceivable interest of the Performing  Arts politics community service among  them you will have complete freedom to  explore to learn about new subjects to  meet new people and to pursue new  passions you will have chances to expand  your horizons to widen your scope of  vision to see the world from many  perspectives I want to encourage you in  every way that I can to make the most of  this rare and unique opportunity the  simple truth is we need you in these  times of great uncertainty when we seem  unable to deal with our gravest problems  we desperately need an infusion of  broadly educated citizens and leaders to  join the debates to raise the level of  discourse and to move us in the right  direction we know all too well the  missed opportunities of the past few  years we entered 2009 full of hope that  the world's nations could agree to save  the planet from the scourge of global  warming but the US Congress dead locked  in a struggle that was dominated by the  parochial interests of various  industries and regions and it failed to  act then in Copenhagen efforts to reach  a global agreement were foiled when  neither the United States nor the  developing nations were willing to  compromise for the good of all and only  the European nations saw the big picture  consider our current economic condition  since January 2009 it has been clear  that there's only one way to prevent a  deep and prolonged recession and avoid  persistent high unemployment I'm an  economist by the way we needed then as  we need now a massive fiscal stimulus in  the form of direct job creation not tax  cuts and we need it at the same time to  make a commitment to reduce the federal  budget deficit  dramatically over a period of years not  immediately since that would prolong the  recession but predictably we finally  faced the question of deficit reduction  this summer but ideological rigidity and  parochialism of members of Congress  moved the dialog away from the big  picture and paralyzed us with rigid  simplistic formulas no tax increases no  increase in the debt ceiling no cuts to  Social Security no cuts to Medicare  after weeks of deadlock we took only a  tiny step forward last December david  Leonhardt a member of the yale college  class of nineteen ninety-four identified  with crystal clarity the issues we face  in the form of a picture that he  published in the new york times i call  it a budget su doku in this brilliant  graphical display he demonstrated that  no combination of discretionary spending  cuts could close the federal budget gap  by 2020 the only way to do so involves  some combination of reducing social  security controlling Medicare and  Medicaid the costs of Medicare and  Medicaid reducing defense spending and  raising taxes or at least allowing the  Bush tax cuts to expire David sodoku  allowed his readers to see the big  picture very clearly and he was awarded  for this the Pulitzer Prize I'm going to  make the audacious claim that David's  Yale education had a lot to do with his  ability to see the big picture  he experienced just as you will over the  next four years exposure to a variety of  disciplines in his case mathematics  economics politics and history not to  mention our history this broad education  has allowed him to look beyond the  small-mindedness of what politicians say  to interpret the larger trends driving  the economy and society he also learned  to write clearly analytically and  forcefully he mastered this essential  tool not simply by taking English  courses but also through his principal  extracurricular activity as a reporter  for and subsequently editor of the Yale  Daily News david Leonhardt is one of  many visible examples of the profound  way in which the liberal arts education  that you are about to experience can  help you develop the capacity to see the  big picture by sampling courses across a  wide range of disciplines you will learn  to see problems from multiple  perspectives and by learning to think  critically and analytically you will  become dis inclined to accept simplified  slogans as truth you'll be more able to  see subtle interconnections and more  capable of forging solutions that  embrace complexity without being  overwhelmed by it no matter what  subjects you choose to pursue in depth  you will be required in your first two  years to develop some breath and you  will be challenged to think for  yourselves independently and  analytically in short you will equip  yourselves in the words of Yale  president Jeremiah day writing in 1828  with both the discipline and the  furniture of the mind rendering you  capable of distinguishing clear and  convincing arguments from doctrinaire  assertions and unexamined prejudices you  will learn how to see the forest for the  this capacity to see the big picture has  been a characteristic of Yale graduates  for decades in 1916 as America SAT by  watching a world war that many believed  it would inevitably enter a group of  yale college students came to realize  that the new technology of aviation  might potentially change the shape of  warfare while still in school they  convinced the navy to constitute them as  the first squadron of what became the  Naval Air Reserve in March 1917 these 29  young aviators left school on mass and  enlisted in the Navy immediately  comprising more than one-third of the  military's qualified pilots they made a  substantial contribution to the Allied  war effort because their Yale education  helped them to see clearly what others  could not aviation has a role in the  story of another Yale a Yale graduate  who was capable of seeing the big  picture Fred Smith classic 1966 had a  vision that came to revolutionize the  transportation of documents and small  parcels in the senior essay that he  submitted to the Yale Economics  Department he laid out a simple idea  that shipments might be gathered from  metropolitan areas all over the United  States and flown in the evening to one  central hub where they'd be reloaded  onto planes headed to their destination  areas and then flown out for delivery  the next day now the oft-repeated story  is that mr. Smith earned a C grade for  his efforts and then went on after  serving in the military to found a  logistics company we now know as Federal  Express according to the legend he saw  the big picture even if his economics  professor did not  alas it's not true that he got to see  but it does make the story better in the  telling lest you think that airplanes  always figure into the narratives of  Yale graduates capable of seeing the big  pictures let me give you two more  examples when Ruth to go Leah class of  2003 served as a summer intern in  Guatemala she came to recognize that  women in the rural villages in which she  was working made weavings of unusual  quality and beauty but they sold for  nearly nothing in local markets Ruth  encouraged these women to form  cooperatives and market their wares over  the Internet she brought them fashion  magazines to inspire them to weave  woolens in patterns that would appeal to  North American tastes today the earnings  of the cooperatives that Ruth helped to  establish provide substantial support  through public education as well as  access to electricity and clean water  and public health in more than 30  Guatemalan villages just one more david  levin no relation studied history and  the history of education at yale and he  designed and directed a tutoring program  for New Haven school children while he  was here he then joined Teach for  America and came to an understanding of  why k through 12 education was failing  in our inner-city schools in his big  picture the students needed the  discipline to focus on their work the  self-confidence to believe that they  could succeed and the motivation to  recognize that their discipline would be  rewarded with a better life if they  managed to graduate and attend college  from this inspiration came the Kipp  schools which David founded in  partnership with Michael Feinberg the  graduate of another Ivy League school  hip has been a pioneer in urban  education demonstrating a model that  truly works these stories inspire us  with the achievements of those who  started here just like you  with great potential curiosity  open-mindedness and a desire to make a  difference in the world the  extraordinary resources of Yale College  will give you an opportunity to realize  that potential to exercise that  curiosity to expand the mud that mind  beyond anything you can possibly now  imagine and to fulfill that desire to  make a difference if you stretch to your  limits take courses and engage in  extracurricular activities that broaden  and challenge you then you too will  develop the capacity to see the big  picture we're counting on you you're off  on a grand adventure make the most of it  welcome to you
 

